(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test07_1B"] = [{"id":"5a97d9cd","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"In a study of residential mobility in Washington, DC, LaTanya Brown-Robertson et al. found that increases in household spending power (via tax credits) had mixed effects on mobility decisions, possibly because residential location choices are not fully reducible to economic factors but are also ______ noneconomic factors such as access to strong local social networks.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In a study of residential mobility in Washington, DC, LaTanya Brown-Robertson et al. found that increases in household spending power (via tax","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"credits) had mixed effects on mobility decisions, possibly because residential location choices are not fully reducible to economic factors but are","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"also ______ noneconomic factors such as access to strong local social networks.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["transposed with","mediated by","decoupled from","antecedent to"]},{"id":"5fa165f7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"In the 1960s, Sam Gilliam, a Black painter from the southern United States, became the first artist to drape painted canvases into flowing shapes. He later explored a different style, ______ quilt-like paintings inspired by the patchwork quilting tradition of Black communities in the South.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["predicting","refusing","hiding","creating"]},{"id":"e3ffb854","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from John Matheus’s 1925 short story “Fog.” The fog extended its tentacles over city and river, gradually obliterating traces of familiar landscapes. At five-thirty the old Panhandle bridge, supported by massive sandstone pillars, stalwart, as when erected fifty years before to serve a generation now passed behind the portals of life, had become a spectral outline against the sky. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from John Matheus’s 1925 short story “Fog.”","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The fog extended its tentacles over city and river, gradually obliterating traces of familiar landscapes. At five-thirty the old","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Panhandle bridge, supported by massive sandstone pillars, stalwart, as when erected fifty years before to serve a generation now","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"passed behind the portals of life, had become a spectral outline against the sky.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “supported” most nearly mean?","choices":["Held up","Encouraged","Improved on","Defended"],"underline":"supported","underline_segments":["supported"]},{"id":"c977cfcf","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"According to a team of neuroeconomists from the University of Zurich, ease of decision making may be linked to communication between two brain regions, the prefrontal cortex and the parietal cortex. Individuals tend to be more decisive if the information flow between the regions is intensified, whereas they make choices more slowly when information flow is ______.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["reduced","evaluated","determined","acquired"]},{"id":"417f8dfa","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is from Darcie Little Badger’s 2021 novel A Snake Falls to Earth. Nina is looking at old family photographs. Nina couldn’t stop looking at the sepia-toned photograph in an oak frame. Propped on the window ledge, it featured a portrait of Great-Great-Grandmother Rosita as a young woman. ©2021 by Darcie Little Badger As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Darcie Little Badger’s 2021 novel A Snake Falls to Earth. Nina is looking at old family photographs.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Nina couldn’t stop looking at the sepia-toned photograph in an oak frame. Propped on the window ledge, it featured a portrait of","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Great-Great-Grandmother Rosita as a young woman.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"©2021 by Darcie Little Badger","indent":4}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “featured” most nearly mean?","choices":["Displayed","Questioned","Approved","Ignored"],"underline":"featured","underline_segments":["featured"]},{"id":"b13378c8","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Early in the Great Migration of 1910 – 1970, which involved the mass migration of Black people from the southern to the northern United States, political activist and Chicago Defender writer Fannie Barrier Williams was instrumental in helping other Black women establish themselves in the North. Many women hoped for better employment opportunities in the North because, in the South, they faced much competition for domestic employment and men tended to get agricultural work. To aid with this transition, Barrier Williams helped secure job placement in the North for many women before they even began their journey.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Early in the Great Migration of 1910–1970, which involved the mass migration of Black people from the southern to the northern United States,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"political activist and Chicago Defender writer Fannie Barrier Williams was instrumental in helping other Black women establish themselves in the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"North. Many women hoped for better employment opportunities in the North because, in the South, they faced much competition for domestic","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"employment and men tended to get agricultural work. To aid with this transition, Barrier Williams helped secure job placement in the North for","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"many women before they even began their journey.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To introduce and illustrate Barrier Williams’s integral role in supporting other Black women as their circumstances changed during part of the Great Migration","To establish that Barrier Williams used her professional connections to arrange employment for other Black women, including jobs with the Chicago Defender","To demonstrate that the factors that motivated the start of the Great Migration were different for Black women than they were for Black men","To provide an overview of the employment challenges faced by Black women in the agricultural and domestic spheres in the southern United States"]},{"id":"f3fac04f","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Bioluminescent beetles called fireflies may seem to create flashes of light randomly, but each species of firefly actually has its own special series of repeated flashes and pauses. These unique ______ allow fireflies of the same species to find each other.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Bioluminescent beetles called fireflies may seem to create flashes of light randomly, but each species of firefly actually has its own special","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"series of repeated flashes and pauses. These unique ______ allow fireflies of the same species to find each other.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["quantities","decorations","patterns","agreements"]},{"id":"441e2b9e","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Researchers and conservationists stress that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is ______. For example, people can take simple steps such as washing their footwear after travel to avoid introducing potentially invasive organisms into new environments.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["preventable","undeniable","common","concerning"]},{"id":"1e85caa9","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is from Edith Nesbit’s 1902 novel Five Children and It. Five young siblings have just moved with their parents from London to a house in the countryside that they call the White House. It was not really a pretty house at all; it was quite ordinary, and mother thought it was rather inconvenient, and was quite annoyed at there being no shelves, to speak of, and hardly a cupboard in the place. Father used to say that the ironwork on the roof and coping was like an architect’s nightmare. But the house was deep in the country, with no other house in sight, and the children had been in London for two years, without so much as once going to the seaside even for a day by an excursion train, and so the White House seemed to them a sort of Fairy Palace set down in an Earthly Paradise.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Edith Nesbit’s 1902 novel Five Children and It. Five young siblings have just moved with their parents from London to a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"house in the countryside that they call the White House.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"It was not really a pretty house at all; it was quite ordinary, and mother thought it was rather inconvenient, and was quite annoyed","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"at there being no shelves, to speak of, and hardly a cupboard in the place. Father used to say that the ironwork on the roof and","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"coping was like an architect’s nightmare. But the house was deep in the country, with no other house in sight, and the children had","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"been in London for two years, without so much as once going to the seaside even for a day by an excursion train, and so the White","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"House seemed to them a sort of Fairy Palace set down in an Earthly Paradise.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Although their parents believe the house has several drawbacks, the children are enchanted by it.","The children don’t like the house nearly as much as their parents do.","Each member of the family admires a different characteristic of the house.","The house is beautiful and well built, but the children miss their old home in London."]},{"id":"a15b3219","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Municipalities’ Responses to Inquiries about Potential Incentives for Firm 1,300 1,200 1,100 Number of municipalities 1,000 900 800 700 600 500 400 300 200 100 0 ns e ry tiv e o qui n sp in ce re to d in no de d re n fe o of esp r announcement before election announcement after election In the United States, firms often seek incentives from municipal governments to expand to those municipalities. A team of political scientists hypothesized that municipalities are much more likely to respond to firms and offer incentives if expansions can be announced in time to benefit local elected officials than if they can’t. The team contacted officials in thousands of municipalities, inquiring about incentives for a firm looking to expand and indicating that the firm would announce its expansion on a date either just before or just after the next election.","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Municipalities’ Responses to Inquiries about Potential Incentives for Firm — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"In the United States, firms often seek incentives from municipal governments to expand to those municipalities. A team of political scientists hypothesized that municipalities are much more likely to respond to firms and offer incentives if expansions can be announced in time to benefit local elected officials than if they can’t. The team contacted officials in thousands of municipalities, inquiring about incentives for a firm looking to expand and indicating that the firm would announce its expansion on a date either just before or just after the next election."}],"question":"Which choice best describes data from the graph that weaken the team’s hypothesis?","choices":["A large majority of the municipalities that received an inquiry mentioning plans for an announcement before the next election didn’t respond to the inquiry.","The proportion of municipalities that responded to the inquiry or offered incentives didn’t substantially differ across the announcement timing conditions.","Only around half the municipalities that responded to inquiries mentioning plans for an announcement before the next election offered incentives.","Of the municipalities that received an inquiry mentioning plans for an announcement date after the next election, more than 1,200 didn’t respond and only around 100 offered incentives."]},{"id":"b28dc26d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"The linguistic niche hypothesis (LNH) posits that the exotericity of languages (how prevalent non-native speakers are) and grammatical complexity are inversely related, which the LNH ascribes to attrition of complex grammatical rules as more non-native speakers adopt the language but fail to acquire those rules. Focusing on two characteristics that are positive indices of grammatical complexity, fusion (when new phonemes arise from the merger of previously distinct ones) and informativity (languages capacity for meaningful variation), Olena Shcherbakova and colleagues conducted a quantitative analysis for more than 1,300 languages and claim the outcome is inconsistent with the LNH.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The linguistic niche hypothesis (LNH) posits that the exotericity of languages (how prevalent non-native speakers are) and grammatical","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"complexity are inversely related, which the LNH ascribes to attrition of complex grammatical rules as more non-native speakers adopt the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"language but fail to acquire those rules. Focusing on two characteristics that are positive indices of grammatical complexity, fusion (when new","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"phonemes arise from the merger of previously distinct ones) and informativity (languages’ capacity for meaningful variation), Olena","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Shcherbakova and colleagues conducted a quantitative analysis for more than 1,300 languages and claim the outcome is inconsistent with the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"LNH.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Shcherbakova and colleagues claim?","choices":["Shcherbakova and colleagues analysis showed a slightly negative correlation between grammatical complexity and fusion and between grammatical complexity and informativity.","Shcherbakova and colleagues analysis showed a slightly negative correlation between grammatical complexity and exotericity.","Shcherbakova and colleagues analysis showed a slightly positive correlation between grammatical complexity and fusion.","Shcherbakova and colleagues analysis showed a slightly positive correlation between fusion and exotericity and between informativity and exotericity."]},{"id":"cca6fae9","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Delta 15-N Values in Seagrass Samples from Four Sites on the Yucatan, 2016 – 2017 Site February 2016 October 2016 February 2017 October 2017 Akumal Bay no data available 3.3 2.0 6.3 Mahahual 0.7 no data available 2.5 3.4 Tulum 6.1 5.9 2.3 5.5 Xahuayxol 0.9 0.3 −0.9 1.4 15 Because water from natural, uncontaminated sources is less enriched with the stable nitrogen isotope N than wastewater from human 15 14 activities is, the presence of such wastewater in nature can be detected by examining delta 15-N values (a measure of the ratio of N to N) in plants. Karla A. Camacho-Cruz and colleagues assessed delta 15-N values in the seagrass Thalassia testudinum from sites on Mexico’s Yucatan’ peninsula with intermediate tourism development, including Akumal Bay and Tulum, and low tourism development, including Mahahual and Xahuayxol, throughout 2016 and 2017. The data suggest that the intermediate-tourism sites experienced influxes of human wastewater. However, the researchers concluded that this happened intermittently.","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Delta 15-N Values in Seagrass Samples from Four Sites on the Yucatán, 2016–2017","headers":["Site","February 2016","October 2016","February 2017","October 2017"],"rows":[["Akumal Bay","no data available","3.3","2.0","6.3"],["Mahahual","0.7","no data available","2.5","3.4"],["Tulum","6.1","5.9","2.3","5.5"],["Xahuayxol","0.9","0.3","−0.9","1.4"]]},{"type":"text","text":"Delta 15-N Values in Seagrass Samples from Four Sites on the Yucatan, 2016 – 2017 Site February 2016 October 2016 February 2017 October 2017 Akumal Bay no data available 3.3 2.0 6.3 Mahahual 0.7 no data available 2.5 3.4 Tulum 6.1 5.9 2.3 5.5 Xahuayxol 0.9 0.3 −0.9 1.4 15 Because water from natural, uncontaminated sources is less enriched with the stable nitrogen isotope N than wastewater from human 15 14 activities is, the presence of such wastewater in nature can be detected by examining delta 15-N values (a measure of the ratio of N to N) in plants. Karla A. Camacho-Cruz and colleagues assessed delta 15-N values in the seagrass Thalassia testudinum from sites on Mexico’s Yucatan’ peninsula with intermediate tourism development, including Akumal Bay and Tulum, and low tourism development, including Mahahual and Xahuayxol, throughout 2016 and 2017. The data suggest that the intermediate-tourism sites experienced influxes of human wastewater. However, the researchers concluded that this happened intermittently."}],"question":"Which choice best describes data from the table that support the underlined conclusion?","choices":["Although delta 15-N values were generally higher in Akumal Bay and Tulum than in Mahahual and Xahuayxol, the values were lower in Akumal Bay than in Mahahual and Xahuayxol in February 2017.","Delta 15-N values reached their lowest level in February 2017 in both Akumal Bay and Tulum, but no data were available for Akumal Bay in February 2016, when the values reached their highest level in Tulum.","Although all sites showed considerable variation in delta 15-N values, the values remained relatively constant in Akumal Bay from October 2016 to February 2017 and in Tulum from February 2016 to October 2016.","In Akumal Bay and Tulum, delta 15-N values fluctuated considerably across the three measurements made from October 2016 to October 2017."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["2017","However, the researchers concluded that this happened intermittently."]},{"id":"3190835d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Some businesses believe that when employees are interrupted while doing their work, they experience a decrease in energy and productivity. However, a team led by Harshad Puranik, who studies management, has found that interruptions by colleagues can have a social component that increases employees’ sense of belonging, resulting in greater job satisfaction that benefits employees and employers. Therefore, businesses should recognize that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Some businesses believe that when employees are interrupted while doing their work, they experience a decrease in energy and productivity.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"However, a team led by Harshad Puranik, who studies management, has found that interruptions by colleagues can have a social component","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that increases employees’ sense of belonging, resulting in greater job satisfaction that benefits employees and employers. Therefore, businesses","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"should recognize that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["the interpersonal benefits of some interruptions in the workplace may offset the perceived negative effects.","in order to maximize productivity, employers should be willing to interrupt employees frequently throughout the day.","most employees avoid interrupting colleagues because they don’t appreciate being interrupted themselves.","in order to cultivate an ideal workplace environment, interruptions of work should be discouraged."]},{"id":"e80ba20d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Philosophers note that many people have an intuitive sense that while we ought not to lie, there may be circumstances in which lying is permissible. If this intuition is correct and we lack an inviolable duty to speak truthfully, what grounds opposition to lying in the first place? Japa Pallikkathayil has advanced one answer by appealing to a duty to respect others’ agential interests: the possession of false beliefs constrains agency, and thus we ought not to impede the formation of true beliefs unless doing so prevents a greater constraint on someone’s agency or an otherwise impermissible end.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Philosophers note that many people have an intuitive sense that while we ought not to lie, there may be circumstances in which lying is","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"permissible. If this intuition is correct and we lack an inviolable duty to speak truthfully, what grounds opposition to lying in the first place? Japa","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Pallikkathayil has advanced one answer by appealing to a duty to respect others’ agential interests: the possession of false beliefs constrains","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"agency, and thus we ought not to impede the formation of true beliefs unless doing so prevents a greater constraint on someone’s agency or an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"otherwise impermissible end.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["One potential means of justifying opposition to lying is Pallikkathayil’s argument that we have an obligation to respect other people’s agency that entails a commitment to truthfulness except in certain circumstances.","Many people have an intuitive sense that lying is permissible in some circumstances but lack a principled way to identify those circumstances, and Pallikkathayil’s argument may provide a means of resolving that problem.","Pallikkathayil’s argument suggests that if we have a duty to respect other people’s agential interests and if possession of false beliefs constrains agency, then we have an inviolable duty to speak truthfully.","Pallikkathayil’s argument shows that if our intuition that circumstances may make lying permissible is correct, then it is unclear whether there are any grounds for an opposition to lying in the first place."]},{"id":"39de2206","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"The Post Office is a 1912 play by Rabindranath Tagore, originally written in Bengali. The character Amal is a young boy who imagines that the people he sees passing the window of his home are carefree even when engaged in work or chores, as is evident when he says to the daughter of a flower seller, ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Post Office is a 1912 play by Rabindranath Tagore, originally written in Bengali. The character Amal is a young boy who imagines that the","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"people he sees passing the window of his home are carefree even when engaged in work or chores, as is evident when he says to the daughter","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of a flower seller, ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which quotation from The Post Office most effectively illustrates the claim?","choices":["“I see, you don’t wish to stop; I don’t care to stay on here either.”","“Oh, flower gathering? That is why your feet seem so glad and your anklets jingle so merrily as you walk.”","“’ I’ll pay when I grow up — before I leave to look for work out on the other side of that stream there.”","“Wish I could be out too. Then I would pick some flowers for you from the very topmost branches right out of sight.”"]},{"id":"a05cc490","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"With the development of new technologies that use natural resources more efficiently, the overall consumption of those resources might be expected to decrease. Economists have observed that improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource ______ efficiency gains, lowering the cost of use, may increase demand to the extent that resource consumption ultimately rises.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"With the development of new technologies that use natural resources more efficiently, the overall consumption of those resources might be","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"expected to decrease. Economists have observed that improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource ______ efficiency","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"gains, lowering the cost of use, may increase demand to the extent that resource consumption ultimately rises.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["conservation, though,","conservation; though","conservation, though;","conservation, though"]},{"id":"75eb5242","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Many companies have simplified their products’ packaging designs in recent years, but ______ Researcher Lan Anh Nu Ton and her colleagues designed a research study to find out.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Many companies have simplified their products’ packaging designs in recent years, but ______ Researcher Lan Anh Nu Ton and her colleagues","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"designed a research study to find out.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["consumers actually do prefer this simpler style.","do consumers actually prefer this simpler style?","do consumers actually prefer this simpler style.","consumers actually do prefer this simpler style?"]},{"id":"dddfa043","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Before the Erie Canal was completed in 1825, transporting goods by wagon between New York City and the Midwest took up to forty-five days and cost one hundred dollars per ton. By linking the Hudson River to Lake ______ canal reduced transport time to nine days and cut costs to six dollars per ton.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Before the Erie Canal was completed in 1825, transporting goods by wagon between New York City and the Midwest took up to forty-five days","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and cost one hundred dollars per ton. By linking the Hudson River to Lake ______ canal reduced transport time to nine days and cut costs to six","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"dollars per ton.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Erie; the","Erie (the","Erie, the","Erie: the"]},{"id":"a3e87535","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Julia Alvarez’s 1994 novel In the Time of the Butterflies, a fictionalized account of the lives of the Mirabal ______ can serve as a starting point for those wanting to explore how the rule of dictator Rafael Trujillo has been represented in Dominican American literature.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Julia Alvarez’s 1994 novel In the Time of the Butterflies, a fictionalized account of the lives of the Mirabal ______ can serve as a starting point for","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"those wanting to explore how the rule of dictator Rafael Trujillohas been represented in Dominican American literature.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["sisters, and","sisters and","sisters,","sisters"]},{"id":"db2e480a","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"By the time Hawaiian king Kamehameha III ______ the throne, the number of longhorn cattle, first introduced to the islands in 1793, had drastically increased, and so too had the need for paniolo (Hawaiian cowboys) to manage the wild herds that then roamed throughout the volcanic terrain.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"By the time Hawaiian king Kamehameha III ______ the throne, the number of longhorn cattle, first introduced to the islands in 1793, had","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"drastically increased, and so too had the need for paniolo (Hawaiian cowboys) to manage the wild herds that then roamed throughout the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"volcanic terrain.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["ascended","will ascend","ascends","is ascending"]},{"id":"64a40675","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In 1953, a fellow performer tripped on legendary jazz musician Dizzy Gillespie’s trumpet, bending its bell upward. When Gillespie tested the damaged instrument, he realized that he ______ sound of a bent bell over that of a straight one.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1953, a fellow performer tripped on legendary jazz musician Dizzy Gillespie’s trumpet, bending its bell upward. When Gillespie tested the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"damaged instrument, he realized that he ______ sound of a bent bell over that of a straight one.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["preferred; the","preferred the","preferred, the","preferred. The"]},{"id":"ad729337","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"With its clich éd imagery of suburban lawns and power lines, John Ashbery’s 2004 poem “Ignorance of the Law Is No Excuse” may seem barren terrain for critical analysis. ______ cultural critic Lauren Berlant finds fertile ground in just its first two stanzas, devoting most of a book chapter to deciphering the “weight of the default space” Ashbery creates in this poem.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"With its clichéd imagery of suburban lawns and power lines, John Ashbery’s 2004 poem “Ignorance of the Law Is No Excuse” may seem barren","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"terrain for critical analysis. ______ cultural critic Lauren Berlant finds fertile ground in just its first two stanzas, devoting most of a book chapter to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"deciphering the “weight of the default space” Ashbery creates in this poem.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Likewise,","Nonetheless,","In turn,","That is,"]},{"id":"164a32e7","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Claude McKay (1889 – 1948) was a Jamaican American writer. Songs of Jamaica (1912) and Constab Ballads (1912) are two acclaimed poetry collections that McKay published while living in Jamaica. McKay moved to Harlem in New York City in 1914. He is best known as a poet and novelist of the Harlem Renaissance, a literary and cultural movement of the 1920s and 1930s. His most famous works include the poetry collection Harlem Shadows (1922) and the novel Home to Harlem (1928). The student wants to emphasize Claude McKay’s accomplishments before moving to Harlem.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Claude McKay (1889–1948) was a Jamaican American writer.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Songs of Jamaica (1912) and Constab Ballads (1912) are two acclaimed poetry collections that McKay published while living in Jamaica.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"McKay moved to Harlem in New York City in 1914.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"He is best known as a poet and novelist of the Harlem Renaissance, a literary and cultural movement of the 1920s and 1930s.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"His most famous works include the poetry collection Harlem Shadows (1922) and the novel Home to Harlem (1928).","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Jamaican American writer Claude McKay is the author of works such as Songs of Jamaica (1912), Constab Ballads (1912), Harlem Shadows (1922), and Home to Harlem (1928).","Although he is best known as a Harlem Renaissance writer, Claude McKay had published two acclaimed poetry collections in 1912 while living in Jamaica: Songs of Jamaica and Constab Ballads.","In 1914, Claude McKay moved to Harlem, where he would become known as a poet and novelist of the Harlem Renaissance (a literary and cultural movement of the 1920s and 1930s).","Before moving to Harlem, Claude McKay — — author of the poetry collection Harlem Shadows (1922) and the novel Home to Harlem (1928) lived in Jamaica."]},{"id":"74149724","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: John Carver was one of the 41 signatories of the Mayflower Compact. The Mayflower Compact was a legal agreement among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony. It was created in 1620 to establish a common government. It states that the pilgrims who signed it wanted to “plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia” under King James. Carver became the first governor of Plymouth Colony. The student wants to specify the reason the Mayflower Compact was created.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"John Carver was one of the 41 signatories of the Mayflower Compact.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The Mayflower Compact was a legal agreement among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"It was created in 1620 to establish a common government.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It states that the pilgrims who signed it wanted to “plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia” under King James.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Carver became the first governor of Plymouth Colony.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Stating that its signatories wanted to “plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia,” the Mayflower Compact was a legal agreement among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.","Created in 1620, the Mayflower Compact states that the pilgrims wanted to “plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia.”","The Mayflower Compact was created to establish a common government among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.","The Mayflower Compact had 41 signatories, including John Carver, the first governor of Plymouth Colony."]},{"id":"87d8a2ff","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"During a 2021 launch, Rocket Labs Electron rocket experienced an unexpected failure: its second-stage booster shut down suddenly after ignition. ______ instead of downplaying the incident, Rocket Labs CEO publicly acknowledged what happened and apologized for the loss of the rocket’s payload, which had consisted of two satellites.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"During a 2021 launch, Rocket Labs’ Electron rocket experienced an unexpected failure: its second-stage booster shut down suddenly after","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"ignition. ______ instead of downplaying the incident, Rocket Labs’ CEO publicly acknowledged what happened and apologized for the loss of the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"rocket’s payload, which had consisted of two satellites.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Afterward,","Additionally,","Indeed,","Similarly,"]},{"id":"61c0f7b3","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"In 2017, a research team led by Mary Caswell Stoddard determined the average lengths of eggs produced by various bird species. Gygis alba is a species of bird in the order Charadriiformes. Gygis alba eggs had an average length of 4.46 cm. Gavia stellata is a species of bird in the order Gaviiformes. Gavia stellata eggs had an average length of 7.22 cm.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 2017, a research team led by Mary Caswell Stoddard determined the average lengths of eggs produced by various bird species.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Gygis alba is a species of bird in the order Charadriiformes.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Gygis alba eggs had an average length of 4.46 cm.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Gavia stellata is a species of bird in the order Gaviiformes.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Gavia stellata eggs had an average length of 7.22 cm.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize a difference between the eggs of the two species?","choices":["A 2017 study compared the lengths of eggs produced by an array of different bird species, such as Gygis alba and Gavia stellata.","A 2017 study found that Gygis alba eggs had an average length of 4.46 cm, whereas Gavia stellata eggs were longer, with an average length of 7.22 cm.","The bird species Gygis alba, which belongs to the order Charadriiformes, and Gavia stellata, of the order Gaviiformes, were included in a 2017 study that compared the average lengths of their eggs.","Mary Caswell Stoddard led a research study that determined the average lengths of eggs, including those of Gygis alba birds (4.46 cm) and Gavia stellata birds (7.22 cm)."]},{"id":"23da9791","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Scientists have long sought to determine the origin of glass in Chile’s Atacama Desert. A 2017 study concluded that ancient grass fires had melted the area’s sandy soil into glass. In 2021, a different study revealed that the mineral signatures of glass samples were consistent with the mineral signatures of comet samples collected by NASA. That study concluded that the glass had formed as a result of a cometary explosion close to the desert’s surface. The student wants to describe how scientific understanding about the glass’s origin has evolved.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Scientists have long sought to determine the origin of glass in Chile’s Atacama Desert.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A 2017 study concluded that ancient grass fires had melted the area’s sandy soil into glass.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In 2021, a different study revealed that the mineral signatures of glass samples were consistent with the mineral signatures of comet","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"samples collected by NASA.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"That study concluded that the glass had formed as a result of a cometary explosion close to the desert’s surface.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Scientists have long sought to determine the origin of the glass, with one study concluding that it formed when ancient grass fires melted the area’s sandy soil.","Studies in 2017 and 2021 offered different explanations for the origin of the glass.","Mineral signatures of glass samples are consistent with those of comet samples collected by NASA, according to new research.","A 2017 study concluded that ancient grass fires had caused the glass’s formation, but new research suggests that the glass formed as a result of a cometary explosion close to the desert’s surface."]}];
